+# Primary-component boundary generator for arXiv:1403.0679
+
+> **Current status:** the module and all local gates pass, but this result is not
+> ready to submit.  The required OpenRouter oracle runs made no non-error calls:
+> every bare, structural-hint, and placebo request returned HTTP 403 “Key limit
+> exceeded”.  The error transcripts are retained; no hardness verdict is claimed.
+
+## Profile
+
+| field | value |
+|---|---|
+| track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| native domain | algebra |
+| object regime | rational exact |
+| computational core | polynomial identity |
+| certificate form | sparse polynomial |
+| intended intuition | invariant — gcd residue classes preserved by the band graph |
+| domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
+| proposed shipping preset | `hard` |
+
+## The problem and why it is checkable
+
+The generator is based on Eser and Matusevich, [*Primary components of
+codimension two lattice basis ideals*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.0679).
+It gives the solver a `3 x 2` integer matrix `B`, hence the two binomial
+generators of the lattice basis ideal `I(B)`, and identifies a dependent pair
+of rows in opposite open quadrants.  The solver must return a sparse polynomial
+whose support is the unique minimal monomial generating set of the corresponding
+Andean primary component.  This stays in the paper's native algebraic objects:
+the instance is a lattice basis ideal and the witness is a polynomial over Q.
+
+Definition 2.11 fixes `I(B)`.  Example 3.6 identifies the relevant Andean prime;
+Lemma 4.6 turns its slice graphs into band graphs; Theorem 2.8 supplies the gcd
+residue structure; and Theorem 5.1 gives exactly the monomial boundary.  The
+checker re-evaluates that formula with integer gcd, exact divisibility, and
+sparse-support comparison.  It never reads `inst["answer"]`.
+
+## Why Track B, not Track A
+
+Theorem 5.1 is an efficient explicit algorithm, so a Track A claim would be
+false.  A literal enumerative implementation tests every first exponent on the
+band boundary and costs `O(X)`, where `X` is the largest such exponent.  Across
+eight hard instances it solved 8/8, as expected, with a median **2,327,105 exact
+operations** and **0.107722 s** per instance (19,486,600 operations total).
+After noticing that moves preserve the first coordinate modulo the common gcd,
+one jumps between the 16 eligible residues and evaluates the Theorem 5.1
+staircase in at most 117 exact operations.  Scaling the gcd grows the mechanical
+scan while the certificate remains 16 monomials.  The required oracle evidence
+for whether models actually miss this compression is still unavailable.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+With `seed=731`, the demo matrix and ordered target pair are
+
+```text
+B = [[-2,-4], [4,8], [-8,-9]]
+target_pair = [0,1]   # variables x1,x2
+```
+
+The boundary polynomial is `x2^8 + x1^2*x2^4 + x1^4`, serialized as
+
+```json
+[[[1,1],[0,8]],[[1,1],[2,4]],[[1,1],[4,0]]]
+```
+
+`verify(inst, answer)` returns `(True, "ok")`; deleting the last term returns
+`(False, "one_term_missing")`.  This smallest setting is hand-solvable: one can
+draw the width-four band graph or recognize the two-unit gcd step directly.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | `n` (gcd scale) | terms | direct boundary width at seed 731 | role |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 2 | 3 | 4 | hand example |
+| easy | 4,096 | 16 | 69,960 | oracle ladder |
+| medium | 32,768 | 16 | 559,680 | oracle ladder |
+| hard | 131,072 | 16 | 2,238,720 | proposed shipping |
+
+No preset has been accepted or rejected by an oracle; the first easy call was
+blocked by quota.  Doubling hard to `n=262144` doubles the boundary width while
+leaving the answer at 16 terms.
+
+## Local gate results
+
+| gate | result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | pass: 16/16 planted witnesses verify and JSON round-trip |
+| G2 | pass: five corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass: tagged, fenced model-style answer round-trips; garbage gives `None` |
+| G4 | pass: 0/200,000 structure-aware antichain guesses |
+| G5 | pass locally: demo has exactly 1 valid answer among 840; hard sampled density 0/200,000; reference scan cost above |
+| G6 | pass locally: five attacks each 0/8; reference enumerator 8/8 |
+| G7 | pass: doubled width 4,477,440, still 16 answer terms |
+| G8 | pass: 100/100 invariance and 100/100 certificate-transport checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass: 397 chars, about 100 tokens, 64 atomic integers, 115 intended-route operations at measured seed |
+
+The five failed attacks are a row-magnitude outlier guess, lexicographically
+greedy antichain, 128 random restarts per seed, constant-total-degree ansatz,
+and raw-row-entry step ansatz.  Plants and the third-row magnitudes overlap;
+the only deliberately special feature is the dependent target pair required by
+the problem and explicitly disclosed to the solver.
+
+## Oracle and G9 diagnostics
+
+| arm | non-error solved/attempts | retained calls | result |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | 0/0 | 4 | all HTTP 403 quota errors |
+| structural hint | 0/0 | 4 | all HTTP 403 quota errors |
+| placebo hint | 0/0 | 4 | all HTTP 403 quota errors |
+
+`hinted - placebo` is therefore undefined, not evidence of zero effect.  Once
+OpenRouter quota is restored, rerun the bare ladder in this directory and the
+two G9 arms in fresh scratch directories; those runs overwrite their local
+transcripts.  Then update `G9_RESULTS`, regenerate `selftest_report.json`, and
+replace this table with actual non-error outcomes.
+
+## Use
+
+From the repository root:
+
+```python
+import importlib.util
+
+path = "results/1403.0679/gen_1403_0679.py"
+spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("generator", path)
+g = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
+spec.loader.exec_module(g)
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY["demo"])
+assert g.verify(inst, inst["answer"]) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+After valid oracle evidence exists, emit examples with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 1403.0679 20 hard
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This is a deliberately narrow Theorem 5.1 regime: three variables, one integer
+row multiplier, and a disclosed Andean row pair.  A solver that knows the theorem
+or spots the gcd staircase immediately finds the answer cheaply; that is exactly
+why the family is Track B.  The `P(guess)` prior is uniform over all stated
+fixed-size divisibility antichains in the exponent rectangle.  It measures blind
+guessing after enforcing visible shape constraints, not a language model's
+mathematical prior.  No Gröbner-basis, saturation, or external CAS benchmark was
+run; the reference is the exact band-boundary enumerator implemented in the
+module.  Most importantly, the four-vendor/no-tool oracle requirement has not
+run successfully, so practical hardness remains unverified.
